<Doc
  title="Discipline: the nine rules"
  kicker="Track O — Own your Discipline"
  description="The nine Object Calisthenics rules applied to PHP, C#, Java and C++, with the violation and the fix for each."
  path="/guide/object-calisthenics"
>
  <p class="lede">
    Nine mechanical rules. None of them requires talent; all of them require
    agreement. Together they remove the space where accidental complexity grows.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rules were catalogued by Jeff Bay in <em>ThoughtWorks Anthology</em> and are
    used here as the operational form of “Own your Discipline”. They are intentionally
    absolute — the value comes from applying them even when the exception seems
    obvious.
  </p>

  <h2>Rule 1 — One level of indentation per method</h2>

  <p>
    A method may contain a loop or a conditional, never a loop inside a conditional,
    never a conditional inside a loop. The body becomes another method with a name
    that explains the step.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Rule 1"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'Before', code: phpIndentBad },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'After', code: phpIndentGood },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    The “after” version is not shorter. It is <em>readable at a glance</em>: one
    method resolves, another searches. The name <code>find</code> is the
    documentation the original code was missing.
  </p>

  <h2>Rule 2 — Do not use the <code>else</code> keyword</h2>

  <p>
    Use early return or polymorphism. <code>else</code> almost always hides a
    decision that could have ended the method earlier.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Rule 2"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'Before', code: phpElseBad },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'After', code: phpElseGood },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    In C#, Java and C++, the transformation is identical: invert the condition,
    return or throw, and let the happy path be the straight line at the bottom.
  </p>

  <h2>Rule 3 — Wrap all primitives and strings</h2>

  <p>
    A <code>string</code> that carries an email address is not “a string”. Wrapping it
    gives the value one place to be validated, one place to be formatted, and one type
    that the compiler can refuse when the wrong string arrives.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Rule 3"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'PHP', code: phpEmail },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'C#', code: csEmail },
      { id: 'java', label: 'Java', code: javaEmail },
      { id: 'cpp', label: 'C++', code: cppEmail },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Note what is <em>not</em> here: a getter. Equality is behaviour, so it is public;
    the value is not. Four languages, one identical shape.
  </p>

  <h2>Rule 4 — First-class collections</h2>

  <p>
    Any class that contains a collection should contain <strong>nothing else</strong>.
    The collection gets its own type, and the filtering, ordering and invariant logic
    moves with it.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Rule 4"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'Before', code: phpCollectionBad },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'After', code: phpCollectionGood },
    ]}
  />

  <CodeTabs
    title="Rule 4 — C#"
    tabs={[{ id: 'cs', label: 'C#', code: csCollection }]}
  />

  <p>
    The same shape in Java is a class with a <code>List&lt;Route&gt;</code> field; in
    C++ a class with <code>std::vector&lt;Route&gt;</code>. The rule is about the
    boundary, not the container.
  </p>

  <h2>Rule 5 — One dot per line</h2>

  <p>
    Also known as the Law of Demeter. A chain of calls means the caller knows the
    shape of three objects it does not own.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Rule 5"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'Before', code: phpChainBad },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'After', code: phpChainGood },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Fluent interfaces and builders are the documented exception: they return
    <code>this</code>, so the chain never changes the object being talked to.
  </p>

  <h2>Rule 6 — Do not abbreviate</h2>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Rule 6"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'Before', code: phpAbbrevBad },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'After', code: phpAbbrevGood },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Abbreviations optimize for the writer and tax every future reader. <code>repo</code>,
    <code>svc</code>, <code>cfg</code>, <code>mgr</code> are not vocabulary; they are
    noise with a shorter name.
  </p>

  <h2>Rule 7 — Keep entities small</h2>

  <p>
    At most <strong>50 lines per class</strong> and <strong>10 files per package</strong>.
    The numbers are arbitrary on purpose: they are small enough to force a decision.
  </p>

  <p>
    When a class exceeds 50 lines, it has more than one reason to change. When a
    package exceeds 10 files, it is hiding a boundary that should become a package.
  </p>

  <h2>Rule 8 — No class with more than two instance variables</h2>

  <p>
    Three fields usually mean three responsibilities sharing a constructor. Group what
    changes together into a value object, or split the class.
  </p>

  <Note label="The hard case">
    <p>
      A class with <code>name</code>, <code>host</code> and <code>port</code> is not
      three responsibilities — it is one concept (<code>Endpoint</code>) pretending to
      be three. The rule pushes you to name the concept.
    </p>
  </Note>

  <h2>Rule 9 — No getters, setters or public properties</h2>

  <p>
    An object exposes <strong>behaviour</strong>, not state. Asking for state and
    mutating it elsewhere is procedural programming with classes.
  </p>

  <CodeTabs
    title="Rule 9"
    tabs={[
      { id: 'php', label: 'Anemic', code: phpAnemic },
      { id: 'cs', label: 'Behaviour', code: phpBehavior },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    The anemic version lets any caller create an invalid balance. The behaviour
    version has one place where the invariant can be violated — and it is not public.
  </p>

  <h2>When the rules collide</h2>

  <p>
    Rule 3 (wrap primitives) creates more types; rule 8 (two instance variables)
    removes fields. Together they force small, named concepts — which is the point.
    When two rules seem to conflict, the resolution is almost always “you have not
    named the concept yet”.
  </p>

  <h2>Enforcement</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Tool</th><th>What it can check</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>PHP</td><td>PHP_CodeSniffer, PHPStan, deptrac</td><td>indentation, line length, unused code, layer boundaries</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C#</td><td>Roslyn analyzers, StyleCop, NetArchTest</td><td>naming, complexity metrics, dependency rules</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Java</td><td>Checkstyle, PMD, ArchUnit</td><td>method length, cyclomatic complexity, architecture</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C++</td><td>clang-tidy, include-what-you-use</td><td>nesting depth, function size, include hygiene</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The rules that no tool can check — “one dot per line”, “do not abbreviate” — are
    exactly the ones a review checklist exists for.
  </p>

  <Note label="Pro tip" variant="warn">
    <p>
      Apply the rules by <em>deleting</em>, not by adding abstractions. Most attempts
      to satisfy rule 3 on day one create a value object per string. Start with the
      strings that are validated, compared or persisted.
    </p>
  </Note>
</Doc>
